**Karl path:** New: Information → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`afterReport\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/information/what-happens-after-you-report-housing-pest-problem\` · **Type:** Information

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Page title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Page title:**

What happens after you report a housing or pest problem

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

Learn how a 311 report is reviewed and when an inspector may contact you.

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — supply the parent Agency page by hand; the mockup has no field for it._

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_The Title "Who this information is for" is not in the mockup — this tool supplies it, matching sf.gov/manage-covid-19-schools-childcare-and-youth-programs, which renders the audience as an H3 with that title over a bulleted list. Add this block first, so every authored section below is one Information section block later than its place among the mockup sections._

**Title:**

Who this information is for

**Text:**

- Anyone who filed a 311 report or is waiting to hear back about a pest or housing problem.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

How to follow up on your report

**Text:**

You can check on a report you already made. It helps to keep a few details handy.

- Keep your 311 service request number if you have one.
- Note the date you made the report.
- Contact 311 to ask about the status of your request.
- Tell 311 if the problem gets worse or becomes urgent.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Tenant rights and retaliation

**Text:**

Tenants have rights when they ask for repairs or report housing conditions to the City. We never share your name or contact information.

A property owner or manager cannot retaliate because a tenant reports a housing condition to 311 or a health department.

- [Tenant rights and reporting](tenantRights)

**Links in this text — recreate each with the Link tool, do not paste them:**

- "Tenant rights and reporting" → shape 5 — rich text Link tool → Internal link → "Tenant rights when reporting housing conditions"

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

If a problem is found

**Text:**

If an inspection identifies a violation, Environmental Health will issue a Notice of Violation (NOV) to the responsible parties. The NOV will outline required corrections and set a specific compliance deadline based on severity.

- **Complaint → Inspection → Notice of Violation → Compliance period → Follow-up inspection → Reinspection fee → Citation or Director's Hearing if unresolved.**
- Sewage Backups (48 to 72 hours): Raw sewage backups need immediate correction. The standard 30-day compliance window does not apply.
- All Other Violations (30 days): Most cited conditions must be corrected within 30 days. These include overgrown vegetation, trash piles, rodent harborages, general sanitation problems, minor gaps or cracks, and mold remediation.
- These timelines represent general enforcement standards. Actual correction deadlines are established on the official Notice of Violation based on the severity of the health hazard.
- Extensions: HHVC may offer an extension to the compliance deadline if the property owner contacts the inspector before the deadline.
- Bed Bug Treatment: A property owner or manager must initiate professional treatment within 2 working days of confirmation.
- If a property owner misses the deadline, HHVC may charge reinspection fees. These are $256/hour for inspectors and $234/hour for technicians. Invoices must be paid within 30 days. Unpaid bills will incur $10 or $30 late penalties and 1.5% compounded monthly interest.
- Persistent violations can result in citations, civil liabilities, or a Director's Hearing to recover attorneys' fees and administrative abatement costs.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

What to expect on timing and results

**Text:**

- HHVC cannot promise an exact time or date when an inspector will call you or arrive at your building.
- We cannot promise a specific inspection finding or guarantee that a Notice of Violation will be issued.
- It does not replace a notice, citation, or legal document.
- Complaints are not assigned instantly. 311 routing and HHVC inspector assignment can take a few days and are processed on weekdays.

