exports[`transcript Markdown, every corpus page > article11Guide 1`] = `
"# Health Code Article 11 in plain language — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Report → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`article11Guide\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/report/health-code-article-11-plain-language\` · **Type:** Report

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Health Code Article 11 in plain language

### Date — \`date\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Date:**

August 7, 2026

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — supply the parent Agency page by hand; the mockup has no field for it._

### Spotlight — \`spotlight\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_Image alignment (Side by side / Full width) and Image position (Right / Left) are both required radios with no mockup equivalent — choose them by hand._

_Report shows up to 1 Spotlight. That is a rendering and editorial cap, not one the form enforces._

**Spotlight title:**

Read the full Health Code

**Spotlight description:**

This guide is a plain-language summary, not the legal code. Use the municipal code for the complete and current text.

**Button link — Link text:**

View Article 11

**Button link — destination:**

External URL → https://codelibrary.amlegal.com/codes/san_francisco/latest/sf_health/0-0-0-1890

### Content — \`content\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_Every Body block on this page can live in ONE Body field: Heading 2 auto-generates the table of contents and Heading 3 appears under "See all sections". Do not use Heading 4, 5 or 6 — they never render._

**Body — Heading 2:**

See which Article 11 rules apply to housing

**Text:**

Healthy Housing and Vector Control (HHVC) enforces Health Code Article 11, the City rules about public health nuisances. This guide explains the sections most often used for housing, pest, mold, garbage, and animal-related conditions.

The left column names the code section. The right column explains what that rule means in everyday language. A condition may involve more than one section, and an inspector reviews the facts of each case.

**Body — fold in as a bolded lead-in (Report has no Callout block):**

**This is not legal advice.** The municipal code controls if this guide and the code differ. The City will identify the applicable sections and required corrections in a Notice of Violation.

### Content — \`content\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_Every Body block on this page can live in ONE Body field: Heading 2 auto-generates the table of contents and Heading 3 appears under "See all sections". Do not use Heading 4, 5 or 6 — they never render._

_Table block: choose the header option first, then add a column per heading. Use 2 or 3 columns — more forces horizontal scrolling on a phone — and note that text inside a table is NOT machine translated while the rest of sf.gov is._

**Body — Heading 2:**

Article 11 sections at a glance

| Section | What it covers | In plain language |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Sec. 581(a)** | General responsibility for public nuisances | Do not allow a public health nuisance on property you own, occupy, or control. |
| **Sec. 581(b)** | Specific conditions that are public health nuisances | This subsection lists conditions such as garbage, mold, pests, insects, and overgrown vegetation. |
| **Sec. 596** | Complaints, notices, hearings, orders, and abatement procedures | HHVC can use a formal process to require correction after it finds a nuisance. |
| **Sec. 600** | Penalties for Article 11 violations | A person who does not correct a violation may face penalties under the code. |
| **Sec. 605** | Poison oak and poison ivy removal | An owner may be ordered to remove poisonous plants from their property. |
| **Sec. 609** | Healthy Housing inspection program fee | Owners of eligible apartment buildings pay an annual program fee based on rental units. |

### Content — \`content\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_Every Body block on this page can live in ONE Body field: Heading 2 auto-generates the table of contents and Heading 3 appears under "See all sections". Do not use Heading 4, 5 or 6 — they never render._

_Table block: choose the header option first, then add a column per heading. Use 2 or 3 columns — more forces horizontal scrolling on a phone — and note that text inside a table is NOT machine translated while the rest of sf.gov is._

**Body — Heading 2:**

Garbage, filth, and waste

**Text:**

Report conditions like trash, mold, or unsanitary waste using [Report garbage, mold, or trash](filthReport).

| Health code | In plain language |
| --- | --- |
| **Sec. 581(b)(1):** Accumulations of filth, garbage, spoiled food, unsanitary debris, waste, or decaying animal or vegetable matter. | Do not let trash, rotting food, or filth build up inside or outside a building. Put waste out properly for collection and keep shared garbage areas sanitary. |
| **Sec. 581(b)(3):** Accumulations of waste paper, litter, or combustible trash. | Do not let litter, paper, or flammable trash pile up. Remove it or set it out correctly for collection. |
| **Sec. 581(b)(5):** Material contaminated by animal or human waste, urine, or other biological fluids. | Remove animal or human waste and clean contaminated materials. Waste left in a yard, hallway, or other shared area can be a health nuisance. |

