> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Page title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Page title:**

Report garbage, mold, and overgrown vegetation

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

Tell 311 about clutter, animal waste, or plants that may draw pests to a home.

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [CHOOSE] — page not approved

_U6, narrowed — the mockup names the agency as text, not as a page reference, and Karl requires a page. Choose the parent Agency page: HHVC was approved for its own Agency page (sf.gov/departments--healthy-housing-and-vector-control), so that is the value for HHVC pages rather than Environmental Health or DPH. Those two remain Partner agencies._

1. Choose page: "Healthy Housing and Vector Control"

### Cost — \`cost\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_Choose the Cost radio first (Free, Flat fee, Range, Minimum and up, None) — all five variants end at this same description field._

**Cost description:**

Free

### Things to Know — \`things_to_know\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Block 1 — Title:**

Reporting anonymously

**Block 1 — Text:**

You can report anonymously. 311 does not require your name. We never share your name or contact information.

**Block 2 — Title:**

Mold review threshold

**Block 2 — Text:**

For mold on a wall or ceiling, HHVC can act when the affected area totals at least 10 square feet.

### What to Do — \`what_to_do\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_The mockup heading "What to do" is not typed anywhere — Karl's What to Do panel carries its own label. Each step below becomes one Section block._

_A step with text, a button and a callout becomes ONE Section block whose Section specifics holds a Text, a Button link and a Callout as siblings — not fields on the step._

**Section 1 — Section title:**

Start your report

**Section 1 — Section specifics → Text:**

Use 311 to report an active problem to the City.

If the problem is urgent, report now.

**Section 1 — Section specifics → Button link → Link text:**

Report through 311

**Section 1 — Button link → destination:**

None

**Section 2 — Section title:**

If you rent, give 72 hours when possible

**Section 2 — Section specifics → Text:**

Tell the property owner or manager about the condition.

- If they do not respond or start fixing it within 72 hours, submit your report. It can then be assigned for review.
- Do not wait 72 hours if there is an urgent health or safety concern.

**Section 3 — Section title:**

For mold, identify the moisture source

**Section 3 — Section specifics → Text:**

Mold needs excess moisture to grow. Look for leaks from plumbing, roofs, windows, or wet spots, and for condensation from humid indoor air.

Tell the property owner or manager in writing as soon as you find a leak. Include the date, where the mold is, and where you think the moisture is coming from. Keep a copy of your message.

- For mold on a wall or ceiling, describe the size of the area and whether it is spreading.
- Do not rely on a third-party mold test kit when you report. Describe the visible condition and moisture source instead.

**Section 4 — Section title:**

Describe the condition

**Section 4 — Section specifics → Text:**

Include only the details that apply:

- The address or location
- The condition: garbage, debris, clutter, stored items, animal waste, pigeon droppings or roosting, overgrown plants and brush, or mold
- Whether it is inside, outside, or in a shared area
- How long it has been happening
- Whether you told the property owner or manager, if you rent
- Whether you saw rats, mice, mosquitoes, or other pests nearby
- Your contact information, if you want an inspector to contact you

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

For mold on walls or ceilings, HHVC can act when the affected area totals at least 10 square feet. Photos are not required. Describe the location and condition clearly.

### Accordion title and text — \`supporting_information\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

While you wait: tips to help with the problem

**Text:**

It may take us a few weekdays to get back to you. In the meantime, here are a few things you can do to help with the problem.

- **Contain garbage:** Bag garbage before you toss it, and keep bin lids fully closed.
- **Do not feed pigeons:** Feeding attracts droppings, roosting, and other pests.
- **Clean up droppings safely:** Wet down droppings with a disinfectant and wear gloves. Never dry-sweep them.
- **Trim plants:** Cut back overgrown plants and brush, especially where they touch buildings or fences.
- **Store items off the ground:** Keep stored items on shelves or pallets so pests cannot hide under them.
- **Reduce indoor moisture:** Open windows or run fans in kitchens and bathrooms to slow mold growth.

### Custom Section — \`custom_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

⚠ Inferred mapping — verify the destination before saving.

_Inferred mapping — the field map documents this panel only for supporting/flat sections. Transaction has no generic body stream and this is its only repeatable Title-and-text panel, so verify the destination before saving._

**Title:**

Get help making your report

**Text:**

You can make a report even if you are not the tenant. A friend, family member, advocate, or helper can report for someone else.

- You can ask 311 for help in your language.
- You can ask 311 for a service request number so you can follow up later.

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

You can use 311 to report mold caused by humidity or condensation inside a building. Describe where the mold is, how large the affected wall or ceiling area is, and what is causing moisture.

### Custom Section — \`custom_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

⚠ Inferred mapping — verify the destination before saving.

