"# Report a pest problem, start to finish — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Step by step → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`stepPestReport\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/step-by-step/report-a-pest-problem\` · **Type:** Step by step

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Report a pest problem, start to finish

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — the mockup has no primary_agency field, and Karl requires it. Supply the parent Agency page by hand: HHVC was approved for its own Agency page (sf.gov/departments--healthy-housing-and-vector-control), so that is the value for HHVC pages rather than Environmental Health or DPH. Those two remain Partner agencies._

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

Follow a 311 report from filing through inspection, notice, and re-inspection.

### Intro — \`intro\` [TYPE] — page not approved

⚠ Inferred mapping — verify the destination before saving.

_Step by step has no things_to_know and no page-level cost, so a Transaction page's whatToKnow has nowhere to go here. Intro is the one free-text slot before the steps, which makes it where an audience line ("Who this information is for") belongs on this type._

_Inferred mapping — the field map documents this panel only for supporting/flat sections. Transaction has no generic body stream and this is its only repeatable Title-and-text panel, so verify the destination before saving._

**Title:**

Who this information is for

**Text:**

This page follows one pest report from the moment you file it to the moment the case closes. It links to the page for each stage rather than repeating it.

- Tenants and residents who have reported a problem
- Property owners and managers responding to a report
- Advocates, family members, and helpers following a case

### Steps — \`steps\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_The block 27 mockup karl notes were describing. Unlike Transaction's what_to_do Section, a Step carries its own cost and time, and a Transaction link chooser — which is how a Step by step points at the Transaction pages holding the detail._

_The mockup heading "What to do" is not typed anywhere — Karl's What to Do panel carries its own label. Each step below becomes one Section block._

_Each step is one Step block: its Title, then its Step description (text and bullets together). A step can link to one Transaction page or none, chosen with the page chooser rather than typed._

**Step 1 — Title:**

Report the problem to 311

**Step 1 — Step description:**

Start with 311. Pick the report that matches what you have seen — the condition decides which team responds, not the building.

- [Report rats, mice, and other four-legged problems](rodentsReport)
- [Report cockroaches, mosquitoes, and other insects](insectsReport)
- [Report garbage, mold, and overgrown vegetation](filthReport)
- [Report a problem in an SRO or hotel](sroHotelReport)
- You can report a building you do not live in, and you can report on behalf of someone else.

**Step 2 — Title:**

Check what Healthy Housing can inspect

**Step 2 — Step description:**

Not every housing problem belongs to this team. Checking first is faster than being routed twice.

- [Learn what Healthy Housing and Vector Control can inspect](scopeInfo)
- Heat, water service, wiring, and structural repairs belong to other departments.

**Step 3 — Title:**

Get ready for the inspection

**Step 3 — Step description:**

An inspector will look at the conditions you reported. Being ready shortens the visit and avoids a second one.

If you rent, give 72 hours notice when you can before anyone enters a unit.

- Clear a path to the area you reported so an inspector can see the condition.
- If you gave contact information, an inspector may contact you to ask questions or schedule a visit.
- Have photos of the condition ready, dated if possible.
- Keep any messages with a property owner or manager about the problem.
- An inspector may look at the condition you reported and nearby areas that could be related.
- If you did not give contact information, an inspection may still happen without notice.

**Step 4 — Title:**

Read what the inspection found

**Step 4 — Step description:**

After the visit you find out whether a violation was written. You also learn what it covers and who must correct it.

- [What happens after you report a housing or pest problem](afterReport)
- A notice can name an owner, a manager, a tenant, or more than one party.

**Step 5 — Title:**

Follow the correction work

**Step 5 — Step description:**

The notice sets what has to be corrected and by when. That deadline comes from the notice, not from this page.

- [Correct a violation and close your case](stepCorrectViolation)
- [What tenants need to do after a Notice of Violation](tenantNoticeSteps)

**Step 6 — Title:**

Confirm the work is done

**Step 6 — Step description:**

A case closes when the cited conditions are corrected and that has been confirmed. A follow-up inspection may be needed first.

- Steps 5 and 6 of [Correct a violation and close your case](stepCorrectViolation) cover proof of the work and the re-inspection.

