"# Tenant rights when reporting housing conditions — Karl transcript

**Karl path:** New: Information → Content
**Decision:** no review recorded
**Page key:** \`tenantRights\` · **Slug:** \`sf.gov/information/tenant-rights-and-reporting-housing-conditions\` · **Type:** Information

**NOT APPROVED — do not publish from this transcript.** Approval in this tool is per page, not per field, so nothing below is signed off.

> Every keystroke below is yours to make. This file is an export, not an approval, and nothing here has been written to Karl.

### Page title — \`title\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Page title:**

Tenant rights when reporting housing conditions

### Description — \`description\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Description:**

Learn about tenant protections and where to get help if you are worried about retaliation.

### Primary agency — \`primary_agency\` [FLAG] — page not approved

_U6 — supply the parent Agency page by hand; the mockup has no field for it._

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

_The Title "Who this information is for" is not in the mockup — this tool supplies it, matching sf.gov/manage-covid-19-schools-childcare-and-youth-programs, which renders the audience as an H3 with that title over a bulleted list. Add this block first, so every authored section below is one Information section block later than its place among the mockup sections._

**Title:**

Who this information is for

**Text:**

- This page is for tenants, and anyone helping a tenant, worried about retaliation for reporting a housing problem.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

You can ask the City for help

**Text:**

Tenants have rights when they ask for repairs or report housing conditions to the City.

Retaliation can include trying to evict you, raising rent, reducing services, or threatening you because you reported a condition.

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

This page gives general information, not legal advice. Contact the Rent Board or a tenant support organization for help with your situation.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Tenant responsibilities

**Text:**

Tenants also have responsibilities under Article 11 and the Director's Rules for keeping their home free of pests.

- **Keep your home clean:** Regular cleaning, taking out trash, and not letting food or garbage build up helps prevent pests.
- **Report pest signs early:** Tell your landlord in writing when you see droppings, gnaw marks, live pests, or damage.
- **Cooperate with inspections and treatments:** Allow reasonable access for pest control professionals, inspectors, and maintenance workers when scheduled.
- **Prepare for treatment:** Follow the pest control operator's instructions before treatment. Move furniture, cover food, and clear access areas.
- **Reduce clutter:** Clutter gives pests places to hide and makes inspections and treatments less effective.

**Callout (rich text, no title field):**

Notify your property owner or manager first, and allow at least 72 hours for them to begin fixing it. If the problem continues or the owner does not respond, report the issue through 311. Do not delay reporting immediate health or safety hazards.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

What retaliation can look like

**Text:**

Retaliation is when a property owner or manager punishes you for reporting a condition. It can take different forms.

- Trying to evict you or threatening eviction
- Raising your rent after you report a problem
- Reducing or removing services you normally have
- Refusing to make repairs because you reported a condition
- Threats, harassment, or pressure to drop a complaint

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

What you can do

**Text:**

These steps can help protect you if you are worried about reporting a housing condition.

- Keep copies of repair requests, messages, and any reports you make.
- Report unsafe conditions to 311 so there is a record.
- Contact the Rent Board or a tenant support organization for help with your situation.
- You can ask 311 for help in your language.
- Ask for help if a disability or medical need makes it hard to report or prepare for an inspection.

### Information section — \`information_section\` [TYPE] — page not approved

**Title:**

Where to get tenant help

**Entry 1 — Title:**

SF Rent Board

**Entry 1 — Text:**

Get information about tenant rights, landlord rules, and rent-related questions.

**Entry 2 — Title:**

Get tenant help

**Entry 2 — Text:**

Use tenant support resources if you are worried about retaliation or eviction.

